/* Generated by tools/export-vault-notes.py from the Obsidian vault. Re-run the script; do not edit by hand. */
Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A Brighter Summer Day":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Taipei, 1960. Si’r, a mainlander boy in night school, falls for Ming, the girlfriend of a gang leader. As gang violence escalates and his father is broken by a secret-police interrogation, Si’r loses faith in every authority around him. He tries to hold onto Ming as the one thing that won’t fail him, and when she refuses to be that, he kills her. Yang turns a real 1961 murder into a portrait of a displaced society with no stable ground.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The magnum-opus of Yang’s work, the defining film of the Taiwanese New Wave, and one of the greatest films of all time.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Society corrupted innately good humans\">Society corrupted innately good humans</a></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Youth culture is an imitation of the wider societal forces</span></li><li>The scene when Si’r is talking with Ming in the infirmary and the camera pans to the reflection on the door.</li><li>Power and what it means for justice; power differentials and how they echo each other in various places in society, how groups conflicts also manifest in this way, through the microcosm of middle school life.</li><li>Militarism, and what it does to society. The society is sort of imbued with violence. They find weapons hidden in the rooms of the buildings that they live in.<ul><li>The kids are dressed as soldiers.</li><li>Honey is dressed like Elvis.</li></ul></li><li>What it means to be a good person; honor.</li><li>Si’r: echo of Pierre Bezukhov</li><li>What kind of an influence is Honey? Is he a nonchalant role model? Perhaps the Straussian reading is that he is in fact an uneducated sham, exposing his limited understanding of the world through a semi-profound sense.</li><li>Imitation of American culture<ul><li>There are some universals between Taiwanese teenagers and American teenagers.</li><li>But ultimately, they are like a cargo cult. They are merely imitating American culture fundamentally.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Addendum</h4><p>Perhaps a Straussian reading of parts of the film could be that the film preempts and critiques contemporary wokeism. Si’r believes that it is his duty to change the women around him to be better, to be more just and more secure and better. He believes that he can provide that, but does not understand the simple fact that the world is not receptive. His understanding of the problem is rooted in a sincere sense of justice, but it is corrupted as the violence of the world makes him more and more self-centered and believe that his actions are self-justified.</p><h4>References</h4>","A firm produces at minimum efficient scale when its long run average cost is minimized":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A firm produces at minimum efficient scale (MES) when its long run average cost is at its minimum, meaning that if it produced less, it would not be utilizing its economy of scale effectively, and any more might lead to diseconomies of scale. This is also where the average cost is equal to the marginal cost.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Firms always want to be producing at their minimum efficient scale, since it is the most economically efficient for them.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> The minimum efficient scale is found where the marginal cost ($MC$) is equal to the average cost ($AC$). When the average cost is above the marginal cost, the firm has not reached the full potential of its economy of scale.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition\">Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition</a><ul><li>A feature that MES allows us to note about markets.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a><ul><li>Describes the formation of a monopoly, which can occur when there are relatively large MES.</li></ul></li></ul>","A ranked-choice voting system cannot satisfy all four conditions of fairness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Arrow’s Impossibility Theorem states that it is impossible for a ranked-choice voting system to satisfy all four of the fairness conditions that he identifies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This exposes a crucial flaw with ranked-choice voting.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The four fairness criteria that he identifies are:</p><ol><li><strong>Unrestricted Domain</strong>: voters are free to rank their candidates however they choose.</li><li><strong>Non-Dictatorship</strong>: no one person is able to decide the outcome of the election; e.g., if every other individual ranks $A$ above $B$, then the dictator’s ranking of $B$ above $A$ should not change the result of the election.</li><li><strong>Pareto Efficiency</strong>: consensus rule; if every individual prefers $A$ to $B$, then $A$ would be elected.</li><li><strong>Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives (IIA)</strong>: given rankings, the introduction of a new candidate into the election should not change the relative ranking of $A$ and $B$.</li></ol><p>A ranked choice voting system cannot, Arrow concludes, satisfy all four of these conditions at once.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect\">Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect</a><ul><li>Instant-runoff voting would not be able to satisfy all four conditions of fairness as outlined here.</li></ul></li></ul>","A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A tragic protagonist can be constructed so as to embody the political character of the society that produced the work, so that the traits that drive the plot are also the traits that the audience recognised as their own.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Where this holds, the play is evidence about the self-understanding of its audience and not only about its author, which is what makes tragedy usable as a historical source.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Sophocles builds Oedipus this way. He espouses democratic commitments, acts with speed and rationality, adapts, and reaches for a Promethean scale of action, all of which are the qualities fifth-century Athens claimed for itself.</p><ul><li><strong>Support for democracy</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I thought it wrong, my children, to hear the truth from others, messengers. Here I am myself— you all know me, the world knows my fame: I am Oedipus.</span><ul><li>Shows how Oedipus is coming down from his position of power to engage with the people on a more personal basis. He doesn’t even want there to be a messenger that brings him news. He wants to directly engage with the people.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Your pain strikes each of you alone, each in the confines of himself, no other. But my spirit grieves for the city, for myself and all of you</span><ul><li>Oedipus describes how he pains for the entire city, something that is clearly “democratic” as he thinks for the wellbeing of the entire city.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Speak out, speak to us all. I grieve for these, my people, far more than I fear for my own life.</span><ul><li>Oedipus decides to have Creon reveal the news about Apollo’s  prophecy in the public, clearly showing his democratic nature.</li></ul></li><li></li></ul></li><li><strong>Quickness of action</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Your king was murdered—royal blood! What stopped you from tracking down the killer then and there?</span><ul><li>Oedipus’ dissatisfaction with the inaction of the people around him, by contrast, illustrates his quickness of nature.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I've not been slow with that. On Creon's cue I sent the escorts, twice, within the hour. I'm surprised he isn't here.</span><ul><li>Oedipus had already predicted the correct next course of option before the leader of the chorus even said it.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">When my enemy moves against me quickly, plots in secret, I move quickly too, I must,</span><ul><li>While the leader of the chorus instructs Oedipus to act with caution and deliberation, Oedipus says that he must move quickly, since that is the speed with which his enemies move. This is possibly a critique of the impulsiveness of the Athenians, rather than a compliment, since this impulsiveness is what keeps Oedipus from learning the truth.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><strong>Pragmatic</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">You pray to the gods? Let me grant your prayers. Come, listen to me—do what the plague demands: you'll find relief and lift your head from the depths.</span><ul><li>Oedipus does not believe in prayer to the gods as the solution to the crisis that Thebes faces. This is something that Oedipus comes to reject by the end of the play.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><strong>Mathematical thinking</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">You said thieves— he told you a whole band of them murdered Laius. So, if he still holds to the same number, I cannot be the killer. One can't equal many. But if he refers to one man, one alone, clearly the scales come down on me: I am guilty.</span><ul><li>“One cannot equal many”; clearly shows the mathematical thinking here and the mathematical language being used.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a><ul><li>Part of the reason for writing the play it sees, is for Oedipus to repudiate the diminishing belief in the gods. He uses the idea of determinism to illustrate the dangers of such an idea to the contemporary Athenian.</li></ul></li></ul>","A viable system must be internally consistent":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Practically, any system that could be considered viable must be internally consistent (as much as possible), since if that is not the case, the system is self-defeating, and thus, no longer a coherent system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This framework can be used to analyze many different systems and judge system based on their consistency.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A system is internally consistent if both $\\phi$ and $\\lnot \\phi$ cannot both be proven from some $\\xi$ . This is specifically referring to semantic consistency (since syntactic consistency is assumed in any second-order system above a purely deductive/logical one). If it is the case that a system is actually inconsistent, then by <em>ex contradictione quodlibet</em>, anything can be proven to be true.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>This note is also related to systems in general.</li></ul></li></ul>","An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An action is defined as being voluntary when the agent chooses to engage with the action, with this motivation originating internally. Additionally, even if the final actions are involuntary/compulsory, if it was voluntary action that lead to the involuntary/compulsory action, then the agent is still at fault.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This lays out Aristotle’s definition for when an action is voluntary, which is the other side of the equation (the other side being when an action is compulsory) when determining moral responsibility.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are two specific conditions required for an action to be considered voluntary:</p><ul><li>One is acting of one’s own volition.</li><li>One understands the circumstances and particulars of the action that one wishes to engage in.</li></ul><p>Given that, there are also two possible variations for the voluntary action.</p><ol><li>You choose between chocolate ice cream and vanilla ice cream.</li><li>You choose to help a drowning child.</li></ol><p>The difference between the two is that only the second is within the moral domain. Thus, only the second of the two actions can be considered praiseworthy or blameworthy.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>Actions that are the result of desire or temper are considered voluntary, since our disposition is something that we can control, and our disposition is the reason for certain desires or tempers. Additionally, moral responsibility tracks from motivation. For instance, licentiousness is more voluntary than cowardice since pain distracts the sufferers, and makes their actions less voluntary. In this way, the person who is more cowardly (in pain) is less responsible for the licentious person.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>In order for the agent to act in a virtuous state, they must act of their own volition, i.e., they must act voluntarily.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a><ul><li>The exact opposite of a voluntary action.</li></ul></li></ul>","Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle defines friendship as when two people have affection for each other, wish well for one another, and both mutually recognize their affection for one another. If these conditions are not met, it is not a friendship.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Understanding the definition of friendship is crucial for further argumentation on the matter.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>When applied to the three types of friendships:</p><ul><li>For utilitarian friendships, all three conditions are contingent on the fact that the other person is useful to you. </li><li>For pleasurable friendships, all three conditions are contingent on the fact that the other person is pleasant to you.</li><li>In the case of a virtuous friendship, all three conditions are contingent on the fact that the other person has good character.</li></ul><p>Additionally, friends of virtue don’t need justice between them (not to say that it does not exist, but simply that justice is not needed as an external check). Justice is only applicable between friends of utility.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>Understanding the definition of friendship is crucial for understanding the best form of friendship.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved\">Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved</a><ul><li>While these forms of friendships are lower than the one based on virtue, the definition of friendship still applies to them.</li></ul></li></ul>","Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Genghis Khan was able to take advantage of the fighting ability of the Mongols and pit them towards external enemies rather than focusing on internal squabbling (see Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This, combined with the raw firepower of the Mongol warriors, was what allowed them to take over so much of Eurasia.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe\">The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe</a><ul><li>The structure being unified, and why the strongest leader naturally accrued power.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200\">Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200</a><ul><li>A material condition that made this unified force more effective.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division\">Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division</a></li></ul>","Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Geography can play an important role in determining the way that states can come to form, as different geographic factors can lead to different challenges for civilizations, whether directly as a result of the geography, or due to related causes.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant as geography has influenced the way that many civilizations have formed, and the acknowledgement of geography’s influence can better help us analyze the reasons for specific reasons that a civilizations developed in a specific way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Rashidun conquest was made possible by geography and unique political economy\">The Rashidun conquest was made possible by geography and unique political economy</a></li></ul>","Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is common to observe how once government institutions are created, it is difficult for them to be ended, since there are so many stakeholders, and there is no incentive for efficiency.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that over time, governments can become more inefficient and bloated.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The USDA got involved with the Farmers Home Administration in the 1940s in order to support farmers and improve farming. However, even as farms became big businesses over the next few decades, the USDA continued to work in housing, shifting from farmers explicitly to “rural communities.”</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Activist organizations often shift priorities in order to self-perpetuate\">Activist organizations often shift priorities in order to self-perpetuate</a><ul><li>Related idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Humans are born as blank slates, gaining knowledge a posteriori":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core idea that binds the Enlightenment Empiricists together, namely, that humans are born without knowledge of the world, though with some a priori tools for understanding the world with.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core thesis of the Enlightenment Empiricists, a core group of thinkers that would come to influence later thinkers.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is two aspects of empiricism that need to be differentiated here:</p><ul><li><em>Concept empiricism</em>: that all our ideas trace back to experience.</li><li><em>Judgment empiricism</em>: that all knowledge is justified a posteriori.</li></ul><p>There is far more agreement regarding concept empiricism among the Empiricists than regarding judgement empiricism, where while all agree that we are born with some innate tools to help us understand the world, the nature and scope of such tools is disputed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology\">Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology</a><ul><li>Decartes was opposed to the Empiricist school, being a rationalist.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice as fairness is specifically designed for a closed, well-ordered society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls' theory of justice is specifically designed without the consideration of private associations or international relations. Additionally, it assumes a well-ordered society, which he considers an idealization of actual societies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These are the assumptions that Rawls uses, and thus they can be challenged to challenge the entirety of his theory.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>He makes these assumptions in order so that once a theory based on the assumptions have been established, the theory can more easily be expanded outwards to cover more intellectual ground. He does not claim that this theory holds for all cases.</p><h4>References</h4>","Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Licentiousness (the embodiment of vice) is acting wrongly, but fully conscious and choosing to act in this way. This is due to the licentious person’s understanding of universal morality being corrupted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This helps us understand why some people act wrongly and contrasts this moral state with other moral states.</p><h4>References</h4>","Marginalism is declining in importance as a theoretical framework":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that marginalism is a method of economic that is declining.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has profound for the future direction of economics, and identifies a trendline, a direction of movement for the field of economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Tyler Cowen identifies five reasons that marginalism is declining:</p><ol><li>Empirical science is now displacing theoretical science, meaning that the more theoretical nature of marginalist thinking has been replaced by the data-driven approach of more empirical methods.</li><li>Price theory is declining in importance.</li><li>Finance, which was seen as the ultimate applicant of marginalist thinking, has switched to more empirical science.</li><li>Machine learning is making marginalism obsolete, since it can directly find the structure in data, rather than needing to have a framework like marginalism guide the process of finding the structure in the data.</li><li>Economics is increasingly turning to the left, and people on the left are generally more uncomfortable with the conclusions made by marginalism.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The consumer chooses to consume the amount that makes the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods equal\">The consumer chooses to consume the amount that makes the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods equal</a><ul><li>This is an application of marginal thinking.</li></ul></li></ul>","Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Markets have three crucial functions: they help allocate resources where resources are needed, put savings to use, and help fund new innovations.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the core purpose of markets, and the importance they place in ensuring that capital is utilized effectively to produce the best outcome.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a></li></ul>","Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because people who are older have more experience with particulars, and this experience of particulars is what allows one to develop prudence, older people are generally more prudent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important, since this explains why we typically put older people in positions of power.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Gerontocracies are bad\">Gerontocracies are bad</a><ul><li>Aristotle would disagree with this, since he would consider older people to be more prudent than younger ones.</li></ul></li></ul>","One has to confront their problems head on":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is important to confront problems head on.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that is put forth often in books regarding ethics, or what one ought to do.</p><h4>In <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Essays (book)\">Essays (book)</a></h4><p><em>To consider and estimate the danger is in a way the reverse of being haunted by it.</em> Montaigne urges us to, instead of shying away from a danger, to confront it directly. And so, to deeply consider and estimate the danger is the best way to ensure that you are not affected by it.</p><h4>References</h4>","One ought to be useless":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the best thing one can do is to be useless, since it doing so, once is best able to preserve one’s life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant viewpoint during early Taoist thought.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>People who are too good become powerful, and are thus susceptible to corruption and envy from others, causing problems. People who are too evil are also hated by others and face many problems. Ultimately, it is only be being useless and not being either too powerful or evil that one is best able to live. This does not actually mean actually “doing nothing,” but rather, doing less and acting without artificiality or arbitrariness.</p><ul><li>Doing less is good because overdoing is bad. We should only do what is necessary, and not over-do.</li><li>This also part of Laozi’s critique of the Confucian virtues, which he says are against these principles and therefore against 德 and 道.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite\">To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite</a><ul><li>When we act, then, we must act with this principle in mind.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has no other obligations except to oneself\">One has no other obligations except to oneself</a><ul><li>Since this is true, we can derive the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should desire less\">We should desire less</a><ul><li>Ultimately, we should just desire less, and that would just make us do less.</li></ul></li></ul>","Open-sourcing maximizes reputation per unit of capability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Many AI companies in China open-source their models in order to maximize their reputation with their limited capability.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the strategy that Chinese AI firms have adopted in order to maximize their success.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The reason that so many Chinese firms open-source their models is that the only way the models would get distribution is if they were open source. However, this would change if the models were to improve in quality as to catch up to American ones.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>It seems that some AI firms have already begun to abandon the open-source model.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Quote</p><p>Open source is a phase and a tier. There is evidence that this is already starting to change for some. Zhipu/Z.ai delayed the release of the GLM 5.3 weights. Kimi and MiniMax have both started attaching revenue thresholds to their licenses.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is a structural gap between Chinese and US AI capabilities\">There is a structural gap between Chinese and US AI capabilities</a><ul><li>It seems that if there were no such gap, then the open-source model would eventually be completely phased out (this might occur regardless, but for other reasons).</li></ul></li></ul>","Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem since our globe is in fact a commons, and while it may cost a nation a significant amount in order to mitigate an existential risk, the benefits accrue to all other members of the planet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the impossibility of solving the issue of existential risks without some strong form of global cooperation in order to eliminate free-riders.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Another way to think about this is that there is an over-supply of risk, since the benefit of the risk can be localized, but the cost cannot (it creates a negative externality). When it comes to existential risks, there is also the added temporal dimension, since most of the effects of the risks concerns our descendants, not us right now. Therefore, one can consider it actually an <em>intergenerational global public good</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation\">A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation</a><ul><li>This is perhaps important when considering solutions to the problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","Ran":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows the aging warlord Hidetora Ichimonji, who divides his kingdom among his three sons, expecting a peaceful retirement. Instead, his sons betray and fight one another for power, driving Hidetora into madness and exile. As the kingdom collapses through violence, treachery, and revenge, the film portrays the destructive consequences of pride, ambition, and familial disloyalty.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A film that addresses themes regarding the nature of humanity, and the ability for power to corrupt.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are a product of their times and circumstance\">People are a product of their times and circumstance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Power corrupts those who possess it\">Power corrupts those who possess it</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Remedies to meritocracy either widen access to the competition or reduce what the competition allocates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Proposals for repairing meritocratic societies fall into two families: those that make the contest fairer by removing barriers to entry, and those that make the contest matter less by taking goods out of its reach.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The division is useful because the two families have opposite implications for whether meritocracy is worth keeping, and proposals are often advanced without saying which family they belong to.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Widening access includes removing barriers to higher education and withdrawing the privileges of incumbent institutions. Shrinking the stakes includes distributing non-market goods on non-meritocratic criteria and moving goods out of the market altogether. Only the first family is a defence of meritocracy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Equality of opportunity has a formal and a substantive sense, and meritocracy requires both\">Equality of opportunity has a formal and a substantive sense, and meritocracy requires both</a><ul><li>What the first family of remedies is trying to secure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly\">Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly</a><ul><li>The finding that motivates the second family.</li></ul></li></ul>","Socialism cannot deal with change":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even if socialism somehow manages to solve the knowledge and economic calculation problems, it still faces a problem of dealing with change. To be able to adequately deal with change, they would have to constantly recalculate the preferences of people and the marginal value of capital.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major problem which faces socialism is economic calculation, but solving that, there is still this problem.</p><h4>References</h4>","Successful rebellions need a constructive element":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Successful rebellions require a constructive element other than the merely destructive element in order to be successful.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to be the case when it comes to successful historical revolutions.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The Taiping Rebellion, although it was ultimately brought down for unrelated reasons, was able to survive for a long time due to its ability to build a successful alternative to the Qing state in the regions that it conquered.</li><li>The American Revolution was able to build a successful alternative to the British one that came before it, offering representation and more freedoms to the states.</li><li>Spartacus’ rebellion failed since while he was a capable military commander, he did not have a clear goal, and failed to be constructive in that sense.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The claim that an agent could have done otherwise if they had chosen otherwise analyses the freedom of the action, not the freedom of the choice, so it leaves the disputed question untouched.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the standard objection that forces compatibilists off the simple conditional and toward a categorical account of ability.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is a second and more damaging problem. Consider someone with a severe phobia who would never choose to go outside. The conditional is satisfied, since were they to choose to go out they would go out, yet we do not want to say they are able to choose otherwise. Satisfying the analysis is therefore not sufficient for the ability it is meant to capture.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical compatibilism locates unfreedom in external impediment rather than in determinism\">Classical compatibilism locates unfreedom in external impediment rather than in determinism</a><ul><li>The position this analysis was built to defend.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past\">The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past</a><ul><li>The problem that the categorical replacement then inherits.</li></ul></li></ul>","The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The overall goal of Hinduism is for its adherent to achieve Brahman, or the ultimate state of being.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the overall goal of Hinduism, one of the world’s major religions.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Moksha could be under taken in many different ways, depending on the person and their caste.</li><li>The chief goal of humankind is to achieve union with Brahman, the fundamental reality that transcends the duality of existence and non-existence, through a process known as <em>moksha</em>.</li><li>The process to achieving union with the Brahman is undertaken through multiple lifetimes, through a process known as <em>samara</em>. This is an example of how Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems\">Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems</a></li></ul>","The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of efficiency is not able to mitigate natural contingencies, which means that interpretations of the second principle of justice must appeal to some other conception of “everyone’s advantage.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The principle of efficiency is not able to account for things such as natural-born intelligence or creativity, which Rawls deems unacceptable. He thus prefers the difference principle.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The parallel objection. Careers open to talents fails on both social and natural contingencies; efficiency fails on the natural ones.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>This is also part of the reason why meritocracy is argued against, is that it is able to cope with the existence of natural contingencies, though some formulations of meritocracy are able to do so.</li></ul></li></ul>","The second derivative test allows for the classification of critical points":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If the second derivative at a critical point is greater than zero, then the graph of the function is concave up, indicating a local minimum. If the second derivative at the critical point is less than zero, the graph is concave down, or it is a local maximum.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The second derivative test is one of the fundamental test in calculus that is used to determine the behavior of a function at a specified location.</p><h4>Mathematical Representation</h4><p>To find the local extrema of $f(x)$:</p><ol><li>Find $f'(x)$, set it to zero, and solve for $x$ to find the critical values.</li><li>Find $f''(x)$.</li><li>Plug each of the critical values found into $f''(x)$. If the resulting output is positive, then the critical value location is concave up or a relative min. If the output is negative, then the critical value location is concave down, or a relative max.<ol><li>If the value found after plugging in is 0, the second derivative test failed. You must use the first derivative test to find the local extrema in that case.</li></ol></li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points\">The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points</a></li></ul>","The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Capitalism was enhanced out a sense of a divine calling, even if the resulting work is not optimal from the standpoint of pure self-interest, as the divine calling advocates for a somewhat aesthetic lifestyle. This connects the origins of capitalism with religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of Weber’s core ideas, that the spirit of capitalism was furthered out of sense of a religious calling. He uses this has a jumping off point to dive deeper and elucidate the precise religious origins of this concept.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Money is considered a great danger. Therefore, it is morally suspect and the individual must always act rationally and frugally, living an ascetic lifestyle.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">James William Barclay, according to Max Weber</p><p>The campaign against the temptations of the flesh, and the dependence on external things, was, as besides the Puritans the great Quaker apologist Barclay expressly says, not a struggle against the rational acquisition, but against the irrational use of wealth.</p></blockquote><ul><li>When acquisition occurs as a means to an end, it is morally reprehensible. When it occurs as the fruit of labor in a calling, it was a sign of God’s blessing.<ul><li>When this did occur, the money was saved, not spent → this created capital.</li></ul></li><li>Unwillingness to work is considered a sign of a lack of divine grace.</li><li>Laboring honestly was considered to be acting “in God’s will.”<ul><li>Even if it is at low wages, it is considered “hugely pleasing to God.”</li></ul></li><li><strong>Capitalism is the best/most effective way to act in line with God’s will.</strong><ul><li>This is partly due to its system of the division of labor, which increases productivity ability.</li></ul></li><li>Inequality was represented as “Divine Providence,” that those who were wealthier were more favored by God. This helped justify inequality.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a></li></ul>","The world is unfair and amoral":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The world does not care about what is fair. One’s luck is entirely independent from their moral status/virtuousness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in many philosophies and or works of literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The figure of Oedipus, all that he has to suffer when he finds out about his true nature, was not his fault. In fact, he saved the city of Thebes from the Sphinx. Yet, he is not recompensed with his service, rather arbitrarily punished despite his good deeds.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li></ul>","Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It the statutory incidence of a tax does not matter, since the ultimate market outcome will be the same regardless of if it is tax on the consumers vs. the producers.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that the demand of whether tax should be on consumers or producers is irrelevant in many circumstances.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>There are circumstances in the real world, including with the feasibility of implementation, ease of tax loop holes, etc., such that this law does not always hold.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic tax incidence is based on relative price elasticity\">Economic tax incidence is based on relative price elasticity</a><ul><li>On whom the tax burden falls is dependent on the price elasticity more than the statutory incidence.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>This is an example of when this principle could potentially be violated, since there is rent-seeking behavior and an inability to actually enforce the tax.</li></ul></li></ul>","To elicit high quality responses from an LLM based on some preexisting content, provide the content in the context window":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When you are attempting to elicit a response from a LLM that is based off some source material (e.g., you are asking it to summarize Shakespeare’s <em>Hamlet</em>), the model can produce a much higher quality response is the text is given in the context window.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This helps one understand how to use an LLM in the most effective way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is a finite amount of compute per token in an LLM\">There is a finite amount of compute per token in an LLM</a><ul><li>Gives the reason this works. Supplying the text spends compute on processing rather than on recalling it.</li></ul></li></ul>","Whether a currency has exorbitant privilege is primarily determined by the existence of a viable alternative currency":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whether is currency can continue to have exorbitant privilege is determined by whether there is a real alternative to that currency extant in the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to understand if a currency dominant currency with exorbitant privilege is able to continue having that privilege.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is something that has been observed in past examples of currencies which have enjoyed exorbitant privilege.</p><ul><li>When the Dutch gilder enjoyed the exorbitant privilege, it was the only nation in Europe that had a modern banking system that allowed it to operate in the way that it did and keep the Netherlands wealthy. <ul><li>This ended when the Bank of London rose in power and the pound was able to overtake the gilder, partially due to the involvement of the Dutch in continental conflicts.</li></ul></li><li>When Britain's pound enjoyed the exorbitant privilege, there was no real alternative to it. <ul><li>It was not until after WWII when the US financial system became globalized and US industrial power become something that was felt all around the world that the dollar became the premier currency.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Soft power is premised on hard power\">Soft power is premised on hard power</a><ul><li>The power of exorbitant privilege rests on the country being able militarily and able to maintain its financial system.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
